# Authoritative production startup order

This is the single authoritative command list for the seven independent Duckets
runtime owners. Start each numbered command in its own PowerShell terminal and
in this order. The runtimes coordinate through verified atomic pointers; do not
replace these commands with guessed per-minute sleeps.

Run this preamble in every terminal:

```powershell
cd C:\dev\ducketz
Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope Process -ExecutionPolicy Bypass
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
```

The checked-in watchlist is the authoritative six-symbol production scope:
`AAPL AMZN GOOG MU NVDA SNDK`. CALL and PUT are derived for all six symbols,
so the option-pricing acceptance scope is exactly 12 routes. `SPY` is a
separate research-only benchmark and must not be added to this watchlist.

Before first v3 startup, run the one-time complete ALFRED backfill. The command
derives its observation and real-time bounds from the earliest eligible 1-day
or weekly decision, all configured lags, and feature freshness. It preserves
the sealed importer receipt and immediately publishes the separate readiness
receipt only after causal coverage and integrity pass:

```powershell
python -m ml.option_pricing_fred --datastore-target pc --backfill
```

The current/revised FRED files remain a monitoring and prospective-rate lane;
they are not historical Loop B evidence. For a datastore created before that
prospective rate bridge, its optional one-time seed remains:

```powershell
python -m ml.option_pricing_admin --datastore-target pc capture-current-rate
```

1. Independent CME/L2 owner:

```powershell
python -m datafetching.cme_runtime `
  --datastore-target pc `
  --max-concurrency 1
```

2. Loop A. Its Databento 1-minute fast lane persists all six symbols and
publishes the exact readiness receipt before 1-second history, derived bars,
technicals, FMP, FRED, SEC, or other slower work finishes:

```powershell
python -m datafetching.orchestrate `
  --datastore-target pc `
  --watchlist datafetching\watchlist.txt `
  --providers databento fmp fred schwab sec `
  --cme-mode external `
  --options-mode external `
  --interval-minutes 15
```

3. Independent daily ALFRED owner. It runs the complete four-series update at
most once per UTC date under the shared ALFRED writer lock, uses bounded
overlap, and republishes verified readiness. It is not part of Loop A's
per-symbol or 15-minute cycle:

```powershell
python -m datafetching.fred_alfred_runtime `
  --datastore-target pc `
  --utc-hour 7
```

4. Active Pricing (logical Loop 3). Each readiness target is handled
idempotently. The supported residual model is a 128-component Nystroem RBF
finite-basis approximation with a Bayesian ridge posterior; it is not an exact
GP. Missing model support uses the causal Black-Scholes mean with a wider
sidecar interval. OPRA is the canonical surface when verified and causally
available; Schwab is recorded as explicit fallback. Live rates require causal
FRED/ALFRED evidence. Pricing waits
once for the configured exact Loop A readiness deadline. If it expires, Pricing
skips that target, leaves the current Pricing authority unchanged, and advances
to the next eligible target without creating an empty terminal artifact. The
separate 1,200-second causal window still rejects stale sources and backdated
predictions; a slow research tail cannot mark a newer still-recoverable boundary
as missed:

```powershell
python -m ml.option_pricing_runtime `
  --datastore-target pc `
  --watchlist datafetching\watchlist.txt `
  --interval-minutes 15 `
  --phase-offset-minutes 1 `
  --bar-readiness-mode required `
  --bar-readiness-timeout-seconds 30
```

5. Independent Options Capture (logical Loop 4). It starts after Loop B's +5
information clock and waits briefly on the target pointer. Its v2 publication
identity is `(provider, symbol, target_snapshot_for)`: a committed target is
reused without another provider call, and divergent duplicate content fails
closed. Production mode constructs one bounded, reconnecting Databento live
adapter for `OPRA.PILLAR` definitions and `cbbo-1s`, scoped to the six parent
  symbols. Its per-target reads select from that shared buffer; they do not create
  six new subscriptions. OPRA capture carries independent evidence clocks and
  does not wait for Loop A, while Loop 3 still requires exact Loop A authority to
  price. Schwab remains explicit broker/fallback evidence. A missing
  `DATABENTO_API_KEY` or adapter startup failure stops this owner clearly; it
  cannot silently become Schwab-only. Neither missing Pricing nor delayed Loop A
  readiness prevents OPRA capture or the allowed fallback request for an
  actionable target. Without exact readiness a fallback Schwab response is
  checksum-sealed under the separate
non-production `options/pending-captures/schwab` authority. The runtime polls
that authority while waiting for later boundaries, reconciles it through the
ordinary committed snapshot contract when readiness arrives inside 1,200
seconds, and never backdates availability. Closed-market discovery does not
refetch or republish an already committed natural target:

```powershell
python -m datafetching.options_runtime `
  --datastore-target pc `
  --watchlist datafetching\watchlist.txt `
  --provider-mode opra-canonical `
  --interval-minutes 15 `
  --phase-offset-minutes 6 `
  --pricing-barrier-timeout-seconds 45 `
  --bar-readiness-mode required
```

6. Directional Loop B. The active v3 feature profile consumes receipt-verified
append-only v3 compact Pricing history and verified ALFRED-vintage macro
evidence. Macro
features are active only for 1-day and weekly contracts, with independent
component clocks and freshness. Valid missing, stale, or uncovered Pricing
rows remain audited null `opx__` inputs. Per-family coverage/freshness gates
must pass before the Pricing family may enter fitting; until then Loop B fits
the corresponding versioned non-Pricing baseline while retaining the failed
gate in its manifest. A column's existence is not activation. Corruption,
unsupported contracts, automation-enabled evidence, or an invalid ALFRED
readiness receipt aborts the whole Loop B publication:

```powershell
python -m ml.prediction_runtime `
  --datastore-target pc `
  --watchlist datafetching\watchlist.txt `
  --provider databento `
  --horizons 1h 4h 1d 1w `
  --feature-profile loop-a-all-bsgp-active-v3 `
  --model-family logistic `
  --calibration platt `
  --round-trip-cost 0.001 `
  --interval-minutes 15 `
  --phase-offset-minutes 5
```

7. Strategy. Exact contract pricing is attached to historical and live
candidates before the profitable-outcome classifier is fit or scored. The
persisted probability is then ranked and displayed by Options Strategies:

```powershell
python -m ml.strategy_runtime `
  --datastore-target pc `
  --interval-minutes 15 `
  --phase-offset-minutes 10 `
  --pricing-mode active
```

Optional maintenance commands are not startup dependencies. In particular,
eligibility/quality reports monitor production and cannot block the valid
Black-Scholes fallback. To inspect them without changing decision flow:

```powershell
python -m ml.option_pricing_admin --datastore-target pc readiness
python -m ml.option_pricing_admin --datastore-target pc diagnose-publications
```

Historical OPRA planning is also maintenance, not startup. These commands are
estimate-only, perform no paid `get_range`, derive request/cluster counts from
the XNYS calendar, and report cost, bytes, storage, and resumability:

```powershell
python -m ml.option_pricing_opra --datastore-target pc
python -m ml.option_pricing_opra --datastore-target pc --research-benchmark
```

Paid execution is prohibited unless the operator separately supplies
`--execute`, an explicit `--max-cost-usd`, sufficient capacity, and an exact
approved authorization record. Startup does not imply that authorization.

To open all seven owners in authoritative order from one PowerShell terminal,
run the checked-in launcher. It opens visible child windows and uses the same
OPRA-enabled Options command above; it does not run either historical OPRA
maintenance command:

```powershell
& .\docs\datafetch-ml\start_all_loops.ps1
```

Stop a runtime with Ctrl+C. Never start a second owner for the same lock.
Provider, migration, recovery, and rollout contracts are documented in
`options/README.md` and the Loop 3/4 audit pages.
